{"author_count":3,"contributors":["Giustolisi, Rosario","Schürmann, Carsten","Sheikhi Garjan, Maryam"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712542"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Electronic Voting","Everlasting Privacy","Participation Privacy","Receipt Freeness"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"receipt free electronic voting from zk snark","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5220/0012140000003555","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work is supported by the Villum Foundation, within the project “Enabling User Accountable Mechanisms in Decision Systems”."],"id":"S01-000.00-712542","identifier":[{"id":"85178616238","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-99292857-f687-4ea6-bbf3-d7a76e135345"],"keyword":["Electronic Voting","Everlasting Privacy","Participation Privacy.","Receipt-Freeness"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-000ECF5BC5FDF6DC9845C12533A83C4A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102342671/121400.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5220/0012140000003555","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rosario","key":"giustolisipau","last":"Giustolisi","locid":"62db65c9-470c-472f-b039-6c4a704ef170","name":"Giustolisi, Rosario","orcid":"0000-0002-2917-9601","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"schürmannpau","last":"Schürmann","locid":"1313519f-2074-41d6-8b79-589648e37211","name":"Schürmann, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4793-0099","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Maryam","key":"sheikhigarjanpau","last":"Sheikhi Garjan","name":"Sheikhi Garjan, Maryam","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5220/0012140000003555","isbn":["9789897586668"],"pages":"254-266","publisher":"SCITEPRESS Digital Library","status":"p","title":"2023 International Conference on Security and Cryptography (SECRYPT)","volume":"1"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85178616238"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-99292857-f687-4ea6-bbf3-d7a76e135345"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Receipt-Free Electronic Voting from zk-SNARK","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712542","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Session types specify the protocols that communicating processes must follow in a concurrent system. When composing two or more processes, a session typing system must check whether such processes are compatible, i.e., that all sent messages are eventually received and no deadlock ever occurs. After the propositions-as-types paradigm, relating session types to linear logic, previous work has shown that duality, in the binary case, and more generally coherence, in the multiparty case, are sufficient syntactic conditions to guarantee compatibility for two or more processes, yet do not characterise all compatible set of processes. In this work, we generalise duality/coherence to a notion of forwarder compatibility. Forwarders are specified as a restricted family of proofs in linear logic, therefore defining a specific set of processes that can act as middleware by transfering messages without using them. As such, they can guide a network of processes to execute asynchronously. Our main result establishes forwarder compatibility as a sufficient and necessary condition to fully capture all well-typed multiparty compatible processes.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Carbone, Marco","Marin, Sonia","Schürmann, Carsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712558"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Birmingham"],"keyword":["Linear Logic","Process Compatibility","Session types"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a logical interpretation of asynchronous multiparty compatibility","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.16240","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-712558","identifier":[{"id":"85175796960","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-db25bd1d-2b75-4c3e-a092-486e2ae109c7"],"keyword":["Linear logic","Process Compatibility","Session Types"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-90CA0AB446D772ED23B0B098EC9AB491"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.16240","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of birmingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Birmingham","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"carbonepau","last":"Carbone","locid":"9027b41a-4159-4db7-9ddd-8fc8f41f1fb3","name":"Carbone, Marco","orcid":"0000-0001-9479-2632","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sonia","key":"marinpau","last":"Marin","name":"Marin, Sonia","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"schürmannpau","last":"Schürmann","locid":"1313519f-2074-41d6-8b79-589648e37211","name":"Schürmann, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4793-0099","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-45784-5_7","eisbn":["9783031457845"],"isbn":["9783031457838"],"issn":["03029743"],"publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"A Logical Interpretation of Asynchronous Multiparty Compatibility","volume":"14330"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85175796960"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-db25bd1d-2b75-4c3e-a092-486e2ae109c7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Logical Interpretation of Asynchronous Multiparty Compatibility","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712558","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Cybersecurity concerns are increasingly growing across different sectors globally, yet security education remains a challenge. As such, many of the current proposals suffer from drawbacks, such as failing to engage users or to provide them with actionable guidelines on how to protect their security assets in practice. In this work, we propose an approach for designing security trainings from an adversarial perspective, where the audience learns about the specific methodology of the specific methods, which attackers can use to break into IT systems. We design a platform based on our proposed approach and evaluate it in an empirical study (N = 34), showing promising results in terms of motivating users to follow security policies.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Dalgaard, Jens Christian","Kulyk, Oksana","Schürmann, Carsten","Janssen, Niek Abel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718842"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adversarial training","Cybersecurity","Security Policies Evaluation","Security education","User engagement"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"security awareness training through experiencing the adversarial mindset","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.14722/usec.2023.237300","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718842","ids":["itu-36a503c1-dd09-4280-9023-065357cf853c"],"keyword":["Adversarial Training","Cybersecurity","Security Education","Security Policies Evaluation","User Engagement"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-80A57CBD3DD91259E19EA512F7D1424E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.14722/usec.2023.237300","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ndss-symposium.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/usec2023-237300-paper.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Netcompany IT & Business Consulting A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens Christian","key":"dalgaardpau","last":"Dalgaard","name":"Dalgaard, Jens Christian","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oksana","key":"kulykpau","last":"Kulyk","locid":"1dbd1112-eb47-4406-936c-6807e8845610","name":"Kulyk, Oksana","orcid":"0000-0003-4218-1658","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"schürmannpau","last":"Schürmann","locid":"1313519f-2074-41d6-8b79-589648e37211","name":"Schürmann, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4793-0099","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Niek Abel","key":"janssenpau","last":"Janssen","name":"Janssen, Niek Abel","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.14722/usec.2023.237300","eisbn":["1891562916"],"status":"p","title":"Usable Security and Privacy (USEC) Symposium 2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","netcompany it & business consulting a/s"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-36a503c1-dd09-4280-9023-065357cf853c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Security Awareness Training Through Experiencing the Adversarial Mindset","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718842","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"How do you observe the unobservable? The election technology in use in the Philippines are optical ballot scanners called Vote Counting Machines (VCMs) that scan, count, and transmit election re- sults at the close of polls back to the national tallying center. Post- election audits called Random Manual Audits (RMAs) are required by law to take place prior to the result becoming final. In this paper, we explore the idea of replacing RMAs by Risk-Limiting Audits (RLAs) that are efficient, have a high chance of correcting an incorrect election outcome by the means of a recount, and can therefore strengthen public confidence in the election.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Schürmann, Carsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384231"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["E voting","Philippines","Risk limiting audits"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"post election audits in the philippines","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://dspace.ut.ee/bitstream/handle/10062/84432/Final_2.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384231","ids":["itu-ca425ca9-accc-4b89-a2b1-88e94baa9d9c"],"keyword":["E-voting","Philippines","Risk limiting audits"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-24F37C3C400BC7A7718FBDDAA6BD721C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://dspace.ut.ee/bitstream/handle/10062/84432/Final_2.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"schürmannpau","last":"Schürmann","locid":"1313519f-2074-41d6-8b79-589648e37211","name":"Schürmann, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4793-0099","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ca425ca9-accc-4b89-a2b1-88e94baa9d9c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Post-Election Audits in the Philippines","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384231","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Socio-Technical Systems (STSs) combine the operations of technical systems with the choices and intervention of humans, namely the users of the technical systems. Designing such systems is far from trivial due to the interaction of heterogeneous components, including hardware components and software applications, physical elements such as tickets, user interfaces, such as touchscreens and displays, and notably, humans. While the possible security issues about the technical components are well known yet continuously investigated, the focus of this article is on the various levels of threat that human actors may pose, namely, the focus is on security ceremonies. The approach is to formally model human threats systematically and to formally verify whether they can break the security properties of a few running examples: two currently deployed Deposit-Return Systems (DRSs) and a variant that we designed to strengthen them. The two real-world DRSs are found to support security properties differently, and some relevant properties fail, yet our variant is verified to meet all the properties. Our human threat model is distributed and interacting: it formalises all humans as potential threatening users because they can execute rules that encode specific threats in addition to being honest, that is, to follow the prescribed rules of interaction with the technical system; additionally, humans may exchange information or objects directly, hence practically favour each other although no specific form of collusion is prescribed. We start by introducing four different human threat models, and some security properties are found to succumb against the strongest model, the addition of the four. The question then arises on what meaningful combinations of the four would not break the properties. This leads to the definition of a lattice of human threat models and to a general methodology to traverse it by verifying each node against the properties. The methodology is executed on our running example for the sake of demonstration. Our approach thus is modular and extensible to include additional threats, potentially even borrowed from existing works, and, consequently, to the growth of the corresponding lattice. STSs can easily become very complex, hence we deem modularity and extensibility of the human threat model as key factors. The current computer-assisted tool support is put to test but proves to be sufficient.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Giustolisi, Rosario","Schürmann, Carsten","Bella, Giampaolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382849"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Attacker models","Formal methods","Security protocols","Tamarin"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"modelling human threats in security ceremonies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90395545/final.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382849","identifier":[{"id":"85133013524","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-697ce86d-d411-4c89-acde-5859a816c7c0"],"keyword":["Security protocols","Tamarin","attacker models","formal methods"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-77D83162922840016D13B35C3CA22363"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90395545/final.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Information Security and Trust","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rosario","key":"giustolisipau","last":"Giustolisi","locid":"62db65c9-470c-472f-b039-6c4a704ef170","name":"Giustolisi, Rosario","orcid":"0000-0002-2917-9601","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"schürmannpau","last":"Schürmann","locid":"1313519f-2074-41d6-8b79-589648e37211","name":"Schürmann, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4793-0099","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Giampaolo","key":"bellapau","last":"Bella","name":"Bella, Giampaolo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3233/jcs-210059","issn":["0926227X","18758924"],"issn_org":["0926227X"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Computer Security","pages":"411-433","publisher":"IOS Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Computer Security","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85133013524"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-697ce86d-d411-4c89-acde-5859a816c7c0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Modelling human threats in security ceremonies","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382849","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
